.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt EXIT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm exit ,
.Nm exit_group
.Nd terminate the calling process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn _exit "int status"
.In stdlib.h
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn _Exit "int status"
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn exit "int status"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm exit
and
.Nm exit_group
system calls terminate the current process.
Rach2 has no threads, and both calls use the same handler.
Their i386 system call numbers are 1 and 252, respectively;
EBX contains
.Fa status .
The low eight bits of
.Fa status
are made available to the parent through
.Xr wait 2 .
.Pp
Musl's
.Fn _exit
calls
.Fn _Exit ,
which issues
.Nm exit_group
and falls back to
.Nm exit
if it returns.
Musl declares no
.Fn exit_group
function.
The
.Fn exit
function declared in
.In stdlib.h
performs library termination work, including registered exit functions
and standard I/O cleanup, before calling
.Fn _Exit .
That work is not performed by the system call.
.Pp
The kernel releases the process's open files and current working
directory, reparents its children to init, marks it as a zombie, and
wakes its parent.
The scheduler then selects another process.
If none is ready, the kernel waits for interrupts and checks again.
The parent reclaims a normal child's remaining mappings, descriptor-table
storage, process record, and kernel stack when it waits for the child.
.Pp
The death of the normal system init is fatal and panics the kernel.
When the kernel debug shell is the supervisor, a transient init instead
ends and reaps every other user process before returning to that shell.
A process in a kernel operation completes the operation and releases
its locks before the terminating signal takes effect.
A process running in user mode receives the signal on return from an
interrupt.
Kernel threads and the idle process remain.
The scheduler is disabled when control returns to the debug shell.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These calls do not return to the calling program.
They report no errors.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/forktest.c
forks a child that exits with status 42.
The parent waits and checks that the child exited normally with that
status.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
When the debug shell is the supervisor, each exit logs:
.Dl process: exited with status STATUS
The normal init and login-shell system does not print this line;
the shell provides the command status through
.Ql $? .
.Pp
A transient init returning to the debug shell also logs
.Ql "sys_exit: init process PID exited with status STATUS, returning to kernel shell" .
.Pp
It prints the console message:
.Dl Init process exited. Returning to kernel debug shell.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
